{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Unto the land which your fathers possessed, and you shall possess it.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Seder Olam: 'which your fathers possessed and you shall possess it' — the first and second possessions they have; a third they do not have. That is: the first possession, which Joshua consecrated for its time but did not consecrate for the future, they have; and the second as well, which Ezra and his court consecrated for its time and for the future, they have; a third they do not have, for it requires no consecration, since it remains consecrated standing by virtue of Ezra and his court who consecrated it for its time and for all generations. Thus explained my master and teacher R. Shimon, may his memory be for a blessing." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say: 'the first and second possession they have' — for they possessed the land of Israel the first time and the second time: the first through Joshua and the second through Ezra; but they did not possess the other lands. But the third — it will not be their possession of the land alone, but rather the entire world; and this is what it says: 'and HaShem your Elohim will do you good and multiply you above your fathers.' Therefore the verse mentions 'possession' in the first two clauses, while in the third it mentions a virtue that surpasses both of them. And this resembles what they interpreted elsewhere: Why are all the songs in the feminine voice, while the song of the future is in the masculine voice? For it is written (Psalms 96:1): 'שירו לה׳ שיר חדש' — 'Sing to YHWH a new song'; and it is written (Isaiah 26:1): 'ביום ההוא יושר השיר הזה בארץ יהודה' — 'On that day this song shall be sung in the land of Judah.' Just as this female becomes pregnant and gives birth and goes back and becomes pregnant again, so Israel are redeemed and again go into exile; but in the future, once they are redeemed, they will not go into exile again. And furthermore: just as this female takes a tenth of property, so Israel in the first redemption conquered seven nations, which are one-tenth of the seventy nations; but in the time to come they are destined to inherit the entire world, like a male who inherits all of his father's property, as it is written (Ezekiel 48:33): 'שער יששכר אחד שער זבולן אחד' — 'the gate of Issachar one, the gate of Zebulun one.'" "<b>And in the Midrash</b> Tanchuma, at the end of parashat Shofetim, on (Zechariah 13:8): 'and the third shall be left therein' — that they will not settle in their land except in the third redemption. The first redemption — this is the redemption from Egypt; the second redemption — this is the redemption of Ezra; the third has no interruption. End quote. And further in Devarim Rabbah, on (Deuteronomy 1:8): 'to give them and to their seed after them' — 'to give them,' these are those who came up from Egypt; 'and to their seed,' these are those who came up from Babylon; 'after them,' for the days of the Messiah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {2 "The citation 'in the Midrash Tanchuma at the end of parashat Shofetim' is given as printed; the verse Zechariah 13:8 is cited as the prooftext for the word 'and the third.' The last clause 'after them, for the days of the Messiah' reads the final word of the verse as a distinct referent."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:12.350913009Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}